\f3\fs26 Shakti was deep in slumber. Visions of his blonde dream-woman filled his head, not with erotic montages or flashes of flesh, but unfamiliar feelings of warmth, safety and belonging. They were strolling aimlessly, it seemed, along a broad uncontaminated beach, holding hands. He had never seen anything quite as splendid and even his unconscious mind found difficulty in comprehending the beauty of its own creation. The white sand was uncomfortably hot and so dry, it squeaked under the pressure of each step, the tiny polished crystals clinging steadfastly to their feet, seeking moisture and a free ride to the cool, clean ocean. There were children splashing about in the shallows and floating around on strange blow-up devices, all laughing and playing in the bright sun with no U.V. suits in sight. The sky was an unblemished deep azure and the sun, a brilliant bright orb raining down a deluge of light and warmth. Shakti felt himself crying. No person alive had seen a blue sky or felt sunlight directly on their skin - the layer bludgeoned the life out of all but the most persistent photons and if you lived above the layer, the Swiss-cheese ozone hardly filtered out anything. Shakti looked at their intertwined hands, drew deep a lung-full of sweet sea air and looked in her face, but dream woman was gone. \
\'93Mother, what are you doing here?\'94 He cried incredulously, desperately trying to disengage his hand from hers, but could not as he was now handcuffed to her. \
\'93Come with me, now!\'94 Said Eloise, dragging him away from the water. \'93You\'92re going to college to become an accountant. Let\'92s go.\'94 In the presence of his mother, Shakti was now a small boy and she was far too strong for him to repel. He could only repeatedly whimper, \'93No, mother, no\'94 as she dragged him away from the beauty and light. He was saved when the Vid-phone burst into life. \
\'93Leave me alone, mother.\'94 Shakti said aloud.\
\'93It\'92s not your mother, moron,\'94 screamed the Commissioner. \'93What are you doing there?\'94\
\'93I had to get some sleep before tonight\'92s show, Sir.\'94 He was now slightly more conscious.\
\'93Did I not send you to investigate a murder?\'94\
\'93Mr. Lee? That was suicide. He shot himself and left a dreadful mess. It\'92s all cleaned up now.\'94\
\'93Only you could manage to conduct a 20 minute homicide inquiry.\'94\
\'93Thank you, sir.\'94\
\'93That was not a compliment, you imbecile. Who processed the crime-scene? Where are the samples, holographs, ballistics? Where?\'94 The commissioner\'92s face had gone from red to blue and was now purple. Steam was shooting out both ears of the livid boss as he sucked in air as loudly and frequently as a charging bull. \'93All you had to do was tick the boxes.\'94\
\'93No samples were taken, sir.\'94\
\'93You are a fucking idiot.\'94\
\'93So I have been told.\'94 \
\'93Don\'92t be smart with me, Lieutenant. This is a stuff-up of monumental proportions. If you were anyone else, your head would roll. You can\'92t cut corners because you want to look good on TV. You wanted homicide experience and I gave it to you. You didn\'92t even have to solve it, shit, there are a hundred others out there just like it, all unsolved.\'94\
\'93I\'92m sorry sir, it looked like a suicide.\'94 \
\'93We will just have to let this slide and hope that there are no questions.\'94\
\'93Thank you, sir.\'94\
\'93You won\'92t be thanking me after this, Alex. There has been a terrorist bombing in Erskintown, followed by an attack on the water infrastructure. No one knows how, but the terrorists let off the bomb and then managed to redirect the water away from the fire.\'94\
\'93A state security investigation, thank you.\'94 Shakti was smiling and could see the award ceremony already.\
\'93No. You are on clean-up duty. Go to Hardy Tower 97, and find out what went on down there. The computer system seems to be up to specs now, but there is a huge hole in data storage. For fuck\'92s sake \'96 get those engineers to pull the finger out and get the water flowing in the right direction. Half the city is flooded and level fives are drinking recyc.\'94\
\'93Level fives drinking recyc? This is serious.\'94 He said grimly and then leapt into life and soon was in his designer finery and out the door. 
\f4\i How am I going to do this? Fuck, fuck, fuck!!
\f3\i0 \
He knew that he was going to need help, but could not for ask help from his police colleagues because there was no way they would give it to him. Competent investigators, the ones who had actually bothered to learn the craft, were sick to death of propping up idiots who could not lead an investigation to save their lives, but could blunder and fib their way through a job interview. A disturbing majority seemed entirely disinterested in work, but were eternally obsessed with jagging that next promotion. They were ceaselessly busy, working on their next application for advancement and wholly unavailable for getting their hands dirty with something as trivial as work \'96 there were just so many promotions to go for. Confidence in themselves was only matched by their inability to do the job, but ridiculous egos had to be indulged and unlikely ambitions realised. It was not unknown for applicants to use a bit of poetic licence in describing the feats and achievements of their paltry careers, but these perjurers relied on a strategy of feeding selection panels with bald faced lies, peppered with bullshit and smothered in a rich, creamy layer of management-speak. Once they struck upon the right formula, they could cynically tweak the bullshit/jargon ratio and any job was reasonably within their grasp. The top brass would never allow the system to be brought into question, after all, that\'92s how they got their jobs.\
To his peers, Shakti was no better than those self-aggrandizing professional job applicants. He had the effrontery to jump from Constable to Lieutenant, a rank that previously did not exist, on the flimsy premise that he his was level 5 and it was unfitting for social inferiors to command him. No-one was really sure where he fitted into the rank structure and all were mightily pissed off that a rank would be created just for him. \
His link-show, Random Camera, was considered a joke and a complete embarrassment to real cops, but it was popular with elite management level citizens and their pudgy, pallid families. The judiciary squawked themselves horse in opposition of a two tiered justice system, but when the revenue began to flow, corporate masters pushed judges concerns aside. Managers loved watching the dirty, the uneducated, the lazy and the poor get what was coming to them. Some genuinely wondered why they just couldn\'92t kill them all off. For reasons only known to the wealthy, they really hated the poor and Shakti had, accidentally, tapped into this sentiment. This was the true reason for his miraculous jump in the ranks and if the entire department thought it was just another example of insufferable cronyism, all the better.\
Shakti only knew one person he could turn to for help. Although they had never, could never, socialise in any meaningful way, Shakti thought of Cracker as a friend. He had never let him down and hardly stolen anything from him which were two things he could not say about any of his colleagues. He had automatically assumed the cops did not steal, but was shocked to learn very early in his career that they knocked-off anything not nailed down. He had expected the odd bad-egg would skim a little money off crooks, but he had not expected that they would so readily steal from each other. Before he contacted Cracker he thought he had better arrange some transport and get some idea of what had been dumped in his lap.
